];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Naked Nutrition Protein Review",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/naked-nutrition-protein-powder-review/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Naked Whey a whey isolate?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Naked Whey is a standard grass-fed whey with 25 g of protein per two-scoop serving, and Naked does not label it an isolate. Naked sells a separate Naked Whey Isolate with 30 g of protein and about 0.1 g of lactose per serving. If regular whey upsets your stomach, the isolate is the version to try."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is in Naked protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The unflavored core products are single ingredients. Naked Whey is grass-fed whey, Naked Pea is yellow pea protein and Naked Casein is micellar casein. Naked Whey Isolate adds sunflower lecithin. Flavored versions add flavoring and sweetening ingredients but no artificial sweeteners, according to Naked. Check each flavor's label for sugar."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Naked Nutrition third-party tested?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Naked says Naked Whey, Naked Pea and Naked Casein are NSF Contents Certified, which covers label accuracy and contaminants such as heavy metals and pesticides. Naked Pea and Naked Rice are also independently tested for heavy metals. These are not sport certifications, so tested athletes should choose a product with NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much protein is in Naked Pea?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Naked lists 27 g of protein and about 120 calories per two-scoop serving of unflavored Naked Pea at the time of writing. It is made from yellow split peas grown in the US and Canada, extracted with water. Flavored versions have slightly different figures, so check the label on the bag you buy."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Naked protein good for weight loss?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Unflavored Naked Pea and Naked Whey Isolate are both about 120 calories for 27-30 g of protein, which makes them efficient for hitting protein targets while cutting. Protein helps with fullness and muscle retention during a diet. Weight loss still depends on your total calorie intake. No powder causes it by itself."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Naked Nutrition sold on Amazon?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Naked Whey, Naked Pea and Naked Casein are all sold on Amazon US in several sizes, alongside Naked's own store. Buy from listings sold by the brand or by Amazon itself, not unknown third-party sellers, to reduce the risk of old stock. Compare cost per serving on the day, because prices move."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Naked protein taste bad?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Unflavored protein is not meant to taste of anything, so expectations matter. Buyers commonly describe unflavored whey as mild and easy to blend, unflavored pea as earthy, and unflavored casein as thick and bland. Most satisfied buyers blend it into smoothies or use it in cooking. Flavored versions are available if you want a drinkable shake."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "naked protein powder",
      "naked whey protein powder",
      "naked nutrition review",
      "naked pea protein"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Unflavored Picks",
    link: "/best-unflavored-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker="Brand Review" badge="Label-Checked" author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Naked Nutrition Protein Review</Fragment>
      One-ingredient whey, pea and casein at fair prices. Here is which Naked powder to buy, what its testing claims actually cover, and who should pick a different brand.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#lineup">The Naked Range</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#whey">Whey vs Whey Isolate</a>
      <a href="#testing">Testing</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#taste">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#compare">Naked vs Other Brands</a>
      <a href="#who">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#use">Using Unflavored Protein</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Naked Nutrition sells the simplest protein powders on the market, and most of them are good value. The catch is that unflavored means unflavored, and NSF Contents Certified is not a sport certification. Buy Naked to blend and bake with, not to sip from a shaker.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict on Naked Nutrition
          </h2>

          <p>
            Naked Nutrition does one thing better than almost anyone: it sells protein powders with nothing in them except the protein. Unflavored <strong>Naked Whey</strong> is one ingredient, grass-fed whey. <strong>Naked Pea</strong> is one ingredient, yellow pea protein. <strong>Naked Casein</strong> is one ingredient, micellar casein. If you want to know exactly what you are eating, or you want a neutral base for smoothies and baking, that simplicity is worth a lot.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is also good value. At list price on the brand's site, Naked Whey in the 5 lb bag works out to about $1.84 per 25 g of protein, Naked Casein to about $1.38 and Naked Pea to about $0.70. Autoship cuts those by around 20%. Those are strong numbers for clean-label powders, and Naked Pea in particular is one of the cheapest grams of plant protein we have come across.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade-offs are taste and certification. Unflavored protein tastes like what it is: milky for whey, earthy for pea, chalky and thick for casein. Most buyers who are happy with Naked blend it into something. And while Naked says its core products are NSF Contents Certified, that is not the same as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport. Tested athletes need to look elsewhere.
          </p>

          <p>
            We rank Naked's products on the label, testing claims and cost per 25 g of protein. We have not tasted them, so any taste comments are summarized buyer feedback. For the wider field of plain powders, see our <a href="/best-unflavored-protein-powder/">best unflavored protein powders</a> roundup, which is where Naked usually sits near the top.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Naked Nutrition Range
          </h2>

          <p>
            Naked's catalog has grown well beyond its original three powders. The protein shelf now includes Naked Whey, Naked Whey Isolate, Naked Casein, Naked Pea, Naked Rice (organic sprouted brown rice protein), Naked Egg (egg white protein), a goat whey, a seed protein, a bone broth protein, a beef protein, a clear whey, a flavored vegan shake, collagen, and the Naked Mass gainers. Most of the core powders come in unflavored and a few flavored versions.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein per serving</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What is in it (unflavored)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">List cost per 25 g (largest bag)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Naked Whey</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Grass-fed whey</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.84</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Naked Whey Isolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey isolate, sunflower lecithin</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.20 (2 lb only)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Naked Casein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">26 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Micellar casein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.38</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Naked Pea</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">27 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yellow pea protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $0.70</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Figures come from Naked's product pages at the time of writing. Most Naked servings are two scoops, which matters when you compare tub sizes with brands that use one large scoop. The 5 lb bags hold about 76 servings, the 2 lb bags about 30, and the 1 lb bags about 15. The isolate is the exception, sold in a 2 lb size only.
          </p>

          <p>
            The flavored versions are where the one-ingredient promise loosens. Chocolate, vanilla and strawberry versions add flavoring and sweetening ingredients, and Naked's flavored lines avoid artificial sweeteners, according to the brand. They are still short labels, but check the panel for sugar if that matters to you. Naked Shake is a different product again: pea protein with MCT oil and natural flavors, 20 g of protein and 3 g of sugar per serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Naked Mass is worth singling out so nobody buys it by accident. Naked says each serving contains 1,250 calories and 50 g of protein, from Naked Whey, Naked Casein and organic maltodextrin. That is a weight-gain product for people who struggle to eat enough. If that is you, compare it in our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-gain/">weight gain protein guide</a>. If not, stay well away.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Naked Powders Worth Considering
          </h2>

          <p>
            Four Naked Nutrition proteins, flagship first, plus one certified alternative for tested athletes. Figures are per Naked's product pages at the time of writing and vary by flavor. Most Naked servings are two scoops. We have not tasted these products, and any taste notes are buyer feedback.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Naked Whey vs Naked Whey Isolate
          </h2>

          <p>
            These two cause the most confusion, so it is worth being precise. <strong>Naked Whey</strong> is a standard grass-fed whey from small non-GMO dairy farms, with cows raised without rBGH or rBST, per the brand. It is not labelled as an isolate. Two scoops give 25 g of protein and 5.9 g of BCAAs. Naked says it is cold-processed without acid or bleach and describes it as non-denatured, meaning less damaged by heat.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Naked Whey Isolate</strong> uses whey from pasture-raised cows in New Zealand, per the brand. The unflavored version has two ingredients, whey isolate and sunflower lecithin, which helps it mix. Two rounded scoops give 30 g of protein and about 120 calories, with zero sugar and only about 0.1 g of lactose, according to Naked. Naked's own comparison makes the point that regular whey carries more lactose.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most people, the regular Naked Whey is the better buy. It is cheaper per gram and the protein quality is excellent. Choose the isolate if regular whey gives you bloating or gas, which is usually lactose at work, or if you want more protein per serving with fewer calories from fat and carbs. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> guide explains when the upgrade is worth paying for.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more distinction: testing claims differ. Naked's pages for Naked Whey, Naked Casein and Naked Pea describe them as NSF Contents Certified. The isolate's page instead says its nutrition values are verified by an independent third party and that it is tested to meet FDA safety standards. Both are reassuring, but they are not the same claim. If contaminant testing is your main reason to buy, the regular Naked Whey has the stronger stated certification.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Testing and Certifications
          </h2>

          <p>
            Naked uses the phrase <strong>NSF Contents Certified</strong> on its core products. According to Naked's product pages, that means the contents are tested for accuracy and purity and confirmed to have no harmful levels of contaminants, including heavy metals and pesticides. That covers the two questions most buyers care about: is the protein figure on the label true, and is the powder clean. It is a good standard for a single-ingredient brand to meet.
          </p>

          <p>
            What it is not is a sport certification. <strong>NSF Certified for Sport</strong> and <strong>Informed Sport</strong> also screen for substances banned in sport and are the programs athletes rely on. Naked's pages do not claim either. If you compete in a tested sport, a single-ingredient powder is already lower risk than a heavily formulated one, but low risk is not documented. Buy a certified product, such as the Transparent Labs alternative on this page.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heavy metals matter most for the plant products. Pea and rice proteins tend to carry more lead and cadmium than whey because plants absorb metals from soil. Naked states that Naked Pea and Naked Rice are independently tested for heavy metals, and Naked Pea is also NSF Contents Certified. That is more than many budget plant brands offer. Our guide on <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> explains what those tests mean.
          </p>

          <p>
            The general rule applies here too: certification belongs to a product, not a brand. Before you rely on it, check the exact product and flavor in the certifier's listing. Flavored versions are manufactured with extra ingredients, and their certification status does not automatically match the unflavored version. Keep your batch number if testing matters to you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cost per 25 g of Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Naked's pricing is unusually transparent. The brand site lists cost per serving for each bag size, and the table below converts those list prices to cost per 25 g of protein so the products can be compared fairly. Naked's autoship takes around 20% off most of these, and Amazon prices move on their own, so treat these as approximate.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
