              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The 5 lb bags are the clear value play. Naked Whey costs about 12% less per gram in the 5 lb bag than the 2 lb bag, and Naked Pea costs about a third less. Unflavored protein keeps well when sealed and dry, so if you use one serving a day, the big bag lasts about two and a half months. That makes the savings easy to capture.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compared with the market, Naked Whey is mid-priced for a whey and very good value for a grass-fed, single-ingredient one. Naked Pea is in a class of its own on price. A daily shake of Naked Pea costs roughly $20-25 a month at list price, against $40-100 for flavored plant brands. Our <a href="/vega-protein-powder-review/">Vega review</a> shows that gap in detail, and our <a href="/best-pea-protein-powder/">pea protein guide</a> ranks Naked Pea against other isolates.
          </p>

          <p>
            The isolate is the only Naked protein that is expensive per gram, and the reason is the format. You are paying for extra filtration and lower lactose. If you do not need those, the regular Naked Whey gets you excellent protein for less.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Taste and Mixing: What Buyers Report
          </h2>

          <p>
            We have not tasted these products, so this section summarizes buyer feedback. Expectations matter more with Naked than with most brands. Unflavored whey tastes mildly milky and mixes reasonably well, and buyers often describe it as close to tasteless in a smoothie. Unflavored pea is the opposite: earthy, slightly bitter and thick in plain water. Many buyers say they would not drink it straight, but it disappears in a fruit smoothie.
          </p>

          <p>
            Casein is the hardest to drink on its own. Micellar casein thickens as it hydrates, and unflavored casein in water is bland and chalky, according to common buyer reports. That same thickness makes it excellent for puddings, overnight oats and protein ice cream, which is how a lot of buyers use it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The flavored versions solve most of this, at the cost of a slightly longer ingredient list. Buyers who want Naked's sourcing and a drinkable shaker shake tend to choose chocolate whey. If you mainly drink protein from a shaker, starting with a flavored version or another brand is wiser than forcing down unflavored pea for a month.
          </p>

          <p>
            Blending tips help across the range. Use a blender rather than a shaker for pea and casein, add the powder after the liquid to avoid clumps, and pair unflavored protein with something that has its own flavor: banana, cocoa, berries, peanut butter or coffee. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-smoothies/">smoothie protein guide</a> covers which bases work best.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compare" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Naked vs Other Clean-Label Brands
          </h2>

          <p>
            Against <strong>Transparent Labs</strong>, Naked wins on simplicity and cost, and Transparent Labs wins on certification and flavor range. Transparent Labs' unflavored grass-fed whey isolate is Informed Choice certified and costs a little less per gram than Naked's isolate at list price, but more than regular Naked Whey. Our <a href="/transparent-labs-protein-powder-review/">Transparent Labs review</a> has the details.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against <strong>Ascent</strong>, the contrast is similar. Ascent's whey and casein are Informed Sport certified and stevia-sweetened, with an unflavored whey option too. Naked's casein is cheaper per 25 g in the 5 lb bag, and its unflavored products are true single ingredients. Our <a href="/ascent-protein-powder-review/">Ascent review</a> covers that range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against other single-ingredient brands like <strong>NOW Sports</strong>, Naked is usually a little more expensive and offers grass-fed sourcing and NSF Contents Certified status in return. Against clean-label premium brands like <strong>Just Ingredients</strong> and <strong>Truvani</strong>, Naked is cheaper and simpler, but less focused on flavor. See our <a href="/just-ingredients-protein-powder-review/">Just Ingredients review</a> and <a href="/truvani-protein-powder-review/">Truvani review</a> if flavor without additives matters most.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against mainstream flavored wheys, Naked is a different kind of product. You give up convenience and flavor in exchange for knowing exactly what is in the bag. That trade suits people who blend or bake, people avoiding sweeteners, and parents who want a plain protein to add to family food. It does not suit someone who wants a dessert-flavored shake after the gym.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Buy Naked, and Who Should Skip It
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy Naked Whey</strong> if you want a clean, grass-fed whey for smoothies, oats and baking, and you value a one-ingredient label. Buy the 5 lb bag once you know you get on with it. <strong>Buy Naked Pea</strong> if you are vegan or dairy-free and want the cheapest honest gram of plant protein available, and you will blend it rather than drink it straight.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy Naked Casein</strong> if you make protein puddings, overnight oats or bedtime shakes and want a plain, cheap casein. <strong>Buy Naked Whey Isolate</strong> only if regular whey upsets your stomach, or you specifically want 30 g of protein in 120 calories. For everyone else, the regular whey is better value.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Skip Naked</strong> if you are a tested athlete who needs NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport. Skip the unflavored products if you want to drink protein from a shaker with water and enjoy it. And skip Naked Mass unless you have a genuine weight-gain goal, because 1,250 calories a serving is a lot.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have kidney disease, are pregnant, have a milk allergy or take medication, talk to your doctor or a registered dietitian before adding a protein supplement. Whey and casein are milk proteins and are not suitable for people with a milk protein allergy. Naked Pea or Naked Rice are the dairy-free options. This review is not medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How to Get the Most From Unflavored Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dose by protein, not by scoop. Naked servings are mostly two scoops, so one scoop gives about 12-15 g of protein. That is fine as a top-up in coffee or oats, but a meal-sized dose for most adults is 20-40 g. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">protein needs guide</a> shows how to set a daily target and how much of it a powder should cover.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the plain powders as ingredients. Unflavored whey blends into smoothies, yogurt and pancake batter. Casein thickens puddings and makes a passable protein ice cream. Pea works in savory dishes, soups and baked goods where its earthiness is hidden. In baking, replace no more than about a quarter to a third of the flour with protein, and add extra liquid, or the result turns dry and rubbery.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pair plant proteins sensibly. Naked Pea is high in lysine and lower in methionine, while rice protein is the reverse. Blending Naked Pea with Naked Rice, or simply eating a varied diet, covers both. At 27 g a serving, Naked Pea already clears a sensible per-meal dose on its own. Our <a href="/whey-vs-plant-protein/">whey vs plant protein</a> breakdown covers how the two compare for muscle building.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store bags sealed, cool and dry, and press the air out before closing. Unflavored protein has no flavors to go stale, but moisture still causes clumping. If you buy the 5 lb bag, decanting a week's supply into a smaller container keeps the main bag closed and fresh.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using one scoop and counting it as a serving</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most Naked servings are two scoops. One scoop of Naked Whey gives about 12-13 g of protein, not 25 g. If you have been using one scoop and wondering why you are not reaching your protein target, that is likely the reason. Check the serving size on each bag.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading NSF Contents Certified as sport certified</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Naked's NSF Contents Certified claim covers label accuracy and contaminants, per the brand. It does not cover screening for substances banned in sport. If you are drug tested, choose a product with NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, and confirm the exact flavor in the certifier's database.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for the isolate without needing it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Naked Whey Isolate costs noticeably more per gram than regular Naked Whey. Its advantages are lower lactose and fewer calories from fat and carbs. If regular whey does not bloat you, the isolate's main benefit does not apply to you, and the standard bag is the smarter buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Drinking unflavored pea in plain water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Unflavored pea protein is earthy and thick, and buyers who try it straight from a shaker often give up. Blend it with fruit, milk or cocoa, or use it in cooking. If you only ever use a shaker with water, buy a flavored product instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Naked Mass by mistake</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Naked Mass is a weight gainer, not a protein powder. Naked lists 1,250 calories and 50 g of protein per serving, much of it from maltodextrin. If you wanted more protein without a large number of extra calories, Naked Whey or Naked Pea is almost certainly what you meant to buy.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Naked Whey a whey isolate?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No. Naked Whey is a standard grass-fed whey with 25 g of protein per two-scoop serving, and Naked does not label it an isolate. Naked sells a separate Naked Whey Isolate with 30 g of protein and about 0.1 g of lactose per serving. If regular whey upsets your stomach, the isolate is the version to try.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is in Naked protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The unflavored core products are single ingredients. Naked Whey is grass-fed whey, Naked Pea is yellow pea protein and Naked Casein is micellar casein. Naked Whey Isolate adds sunflower lecithin. Flavored versions add flavoring and sweetening ingredients but no artificial sweeteners, according to Naked. Check each flavor's label for sugar.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Naked Nutrition third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Naked says Naked Whey, Naked Pea and Naked Casein are NSF Contents Certified, which covers label accuracy and contaminants such as heavy metals and pesticides. Naked Pea and Naked Rice are also independently tested for heavy metals. These are not sport certifications, so tested athletes should choose a product with NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in Naked Pea?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Naked lists 27 g of protein and about 120 calories per two-scoop serving of unflavored Naked Pea at the time of writing. It is made from yellow split peas grown in the US and Canada, extracted with water. Flavored versions have slightly different figures, so check the label on the bag you buy.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Naked protein good for weight loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Unflavored Naked Pea and Naked Whey Isolate are both about 120 calories for 27-30 g of protein, which makes them efficient for hitting protein targets while cutting. Protein helps with fullness and muscle retention during a diet. Weight loss still depends on your total calorie intake. No powder causes it by itself.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Naked Nutrition sold on Amazon?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Naked Whey, Naked Pea and Naked Casein are all sold on Amazon US in several sizes, alongside Naked's own store. Buy from listings sold by the brand or by Amazon itself, not unknown third-party sellers, to reduce the risk of old stock. Compare cost per serving on the day, because prices move.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Naked protein taste bad?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Unflavored protein is not meant to taste of anything, so expectations matter. Buyers commonly describe unflavored whey as mild and easy to blend, unflavored pea as earthy, and unflavored casein as thick and bland. Most satisfied buyers blend it into smoothies or use it in cooking. Flavored versions are available if you want a drinkable shake.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

